9 #uuid "ee20556c-e250-51aa-b85c-fc28797d2901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186513684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514344 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514383 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34621fcb-1a0d-5d3e-9880-09e09830a08f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514388 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514390 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514391 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{13}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c1117fb-96ed-5032-8c50-48772b44cf5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514428 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} & = \\\\ {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{4}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4e1eabf-8b25-5ba2-8660-9b7cb6a5610c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514446 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
